: What is official information? 132\nL303@P10: Complying with the Official Information Act 1982 133\nL304@P10: Advice and training 134\nL305@P10: Requests for Cabinet material under the Official Information Act 1982 134\nL306@P10: Security classifications and endorsements and the Official Information Act 1982 135\nL307@P10: Consultation on Official Information Act 1982 requests 135\nL308@P10: Coordinating Official Information Act 1982 requests 137\nL309@P10: Charging for official information 137\nL310@P10: Review of decisions to withhold official information 137\nL311@P10: Privacy Act 2020 139\nL312@P10: Purpose of the Act 139\nL313@P10: Compliance 139\nL314@P10: Ministerial access to and use of personal information 140\nL315@P10: Role of the Privacy Commissioner 141\nL316@P10: Ombudsmen Act 1975 142\nL317@P10: Providing information to select committees 142\nL318@P10: Production or discovery of official documents 143\nL319@P10: Requests for parliamentary information 143\nL320@P10: Ministers and public records 144\nL321@P10: Creating and maintaining records 144\nL322@P10: Management of records 145\nL323@P10: Deposit of Ministers’ papers with Archives New Zealand 146\nL324@P10: Assessing requests for public access to Ministers’ papers 146\nL325@P10: Deposit of personal records 147\nL326@P10: Disclosure and use of official information by former Ministers 147\nL327@P10: Convention on access to Cabinet records of a previous administration 148\nL328@P10: General 148\nL329@P10: Access to Cabinet records by incumbent Ministers 149\nL330@P10: Former Ministers 150\nL331@P10: Releasing Cabinet documents of a previous opposition administration 150\nL332@P10: Related information 151\nL333@P10: Appendix A\nL334@P10: The Treaty of Waitangi/Te Tiriti o Waitangi 155\nL335@P10: Appendix B\nL336@P10: Letters Patent Constituting the Office of Governor-General of New Zealand 1983 161\nL337@P10-12: Index 171Cabinet Manual 2023\nL338@P12: Foreword xiii\nL339@P12: Foreword\nL340@P12: The Cabinet Manual is the primary authority on the conduct of Cabinet government in\nL341@P12: New Zealand. It covers Cabinet procedure and provides guidance for Ministers, their offices and\nL342@P12: those working within the public service. It is part of New Zealand’s constitutional arrangements,\nL343@P12: documenting the executive’s view of how it functions and the main conventions that operate\nL344@P12: within government. The Manual provides guidance rather than prescribing rules, and generally\nL345@P12: contains settled understandings of government practice rather than leading change. The first item\nL346@P12: of business on the agenda of successive governments has been to endorse the Cabinet Manual as\nL347@P12: the basis on which they will operate.\nL348@P12: This 2023 edition incorporates changes made to government processes since the previous version\nL349@P12: was published in 2017, including updated guidance on the importance of privacy, the modernised\nL350@P12: principles governing the public service, and changes to legislative processes following the passing\nL351@P12: of the Legislation Act 2019 and Secondary Legislation Act 2021.\nL352@P12: Cabinet has approved the content of the Cabinet Manual 2023. I encourage all those working in\nL353@P12: government to consult the latest version and follow the guidance it contains.\nL354@P12: Rt Hon Chris Hipkins\nL355@P12-14: Prime MinisterCabinet Manual 2023\nL356@P14: Preface xv\nL357@P14: Preface\nL358@P14: The Cabinet Manual has been an authoritative guide to New Zealand’s system of central\nL359@P14: government decision making since the publication of the first edition, then known as the Cabinet\nL360@P14: Office Manual, in 1979. This is the seventh edition of the Cabinet Manual. Successive\nL361@P14: governments have recognised the need for guidance to provide the basis on which they will\nL362@P14: conduct themselves while in office. The Cabinet Manual fulfils this need.\nL363@P14: One of the great strengths of the Cabinet Manual is that it is not set in stone: it is updated\nL364@P14: periodically, and as a result reflects changes in political, administrative, legislative and\nL365@P14: constitutional arrangements and language. The content of this edition of the Cabinet Manual\nL366@P14: represents an orderly and continuous development of the conventions and procedures of Cabinet\nL367@P14: government.\nL368@P14: In this edition, some guidance, such as that concerning the principles of the public service and\nL369@P14: consultation process during development of bills, has been clarified and expanded. Other\nL370@P14: guidance has been updated to reflect new legislation, such as the Privacy Act 2020, or to reflect\nL371@P14: changes in practice, like the ability for Cabinet and Cabinet committees to meet remotely if\nL372@P14: required. For the first time, the texts of the Treaty of Waitangi / Te Tiriti o Waitangi are appended\nL373@P14: to the Cabinet Manual.\nL374@P14: The Cabinet Manual focuses on principles. It provides guidance, rather than prescribing rules.\nL375@P14: Detailed guidance and information on Executive Council, Cabinet, and Cabinet committee\nL376@P14: processes is contained in the CabGuide, an online resource at dpmc.govt.nz/publications/cabguide.\nL377@P14: The Cabinet Manual is produced in hard copy, and published online on the Department of the\nL378@P14: Prime Minister and Cabinet’s website. Any substantial updates required before the next review\nL379@P14: will be incorporated into the online version. Users of the Cabinet Manual in hard copy are\nL380@P14: encouraged to check the website for updates regularly.\nL381@P14: The review of the Cabinet Manual has been a significant task for the Cabinet Office over the past\nL382@P14: few years. I would like to acknowledge and thank all those who have contributed to the review,\nL383@P14: whether by revising existing text, or drafting new material. My particular thanks to the Cabinet\nL384@P14: Office team who coordinated the review project, and to our late friend and colleague\nL385@P14: Catherine Parkin.\nL386@P14: The Cabinet Manual is a valuable guide, not just for those working in or with executive\nL387@P14: government, but for anyone who has an interest in New Zealand’s constitutional arrangements\nL388@P14: and the systems and processes of executive government. I know that this latest edition will\nL389@P14: continue to be an important work of reference for all those with an interest in the governance of\nL390@P14: Aotearoa New Zealand.\nL391@P14: Rachel Hayward\nL392@P14-15: Secretary of the Cabinet and Clerk of the Executive CouncilCabinet Manual 2023\nL393@P15: xvi Updates and further guidance\nL394@P15: Updates and further guidance\nL395@P15: The Cabinet Manual 2023 is produced in book form and published online at\nL396@P15: dpmc.govt.nz/cabinet-manual.\nL397@P15: The Cabinet Manual is reviewed periodically. Any substantial updates required before the\nL398@P15: next review will be incorporated into the website version. Users of the Cabinet Manual 2023\nL399@P15: in book form are encouraged to check the website for updates regularly.\nL400@P15: From time to time the Cabinet Office may issue Cabinet Office circulars to supplement or\nL401@P15: update guidance in the Cabinet Manual. The Cabinet Manual 2023 refers to some current\nL402@P15: Cabinet Office circulars. These and other current circulars are available on the Department of\nL403@P15: the Prime Minister and Cabinet’s website dpmc.govt.nz/cabinet-office-circulars.\nL404@P15: Agencies are encouraged to consult the CabGuide, available at dpmc.govt.nz/cabguide, for\nL405@P15-16: current guidance on Cabinet and Executive Council processes.Cabinet Manual 2023\nL406@P16: Introduction 1\nL407@P16: On the Constitution of New Zealand:\nL408@P16: An Introduction to the Foundations of the\nL409@P16: Current Form of Government\nL410@P16: The Rt Hon Sir Kenneth Keith, 1990, updated 2008, 2017 and 2023\nL411@P16: A constitution: What is it?\nL412@P16: A constitution is about public power, the power of the state. It describes and establishes the major\nL413@P16: institutions of government, states their principal powers, and regulates the exercise of those\nL414@P16: powers in a broad way. While all constitutions have these general characteristics, each\nL415@P16: constitution is affected by the national character of the state it services.\nL416@P16: The New Zealand constitution: Its main features\nL417@P16: The New Zealand constitution is to be found in formal legal documents, in decisions of the courts,\nL418@P16: and in practices (some of which are described as conventions). It reflects and establishes that\nL419@P16: New Zealand is a constitutional monarchy, that it has a parliamentary system of government, and\nL420@P16: that it is a democracy. It increasingly reflects the fact that the Treaty of Waitangi is regarded as a\nL421@P16: founding document of government in New Zealand (see appendix A). The constitution must also\nL422@P16: be seen in its international context, because New Zealand governmental institutions must\nL423@P16: increasingly have regard to international obligations and standards.\nL424@P16: The Constitution Act 1986\nL425@P16: The Constitution Act 1986 is the principal formal statement of New Zealand’s constitutional\nL426@P16: arrangements. The Act first recognises that the King – the Sovereign in right of New Zealand –\nL427@P16: is the Head of State of New Zealand, and that the Governor-General appointed by him is his\nL428@P16: representative in New Zealand. Each can, in general, exercise all the powers of the other.\nL429@P16: The Act then deals with the executive, the legislature, and the judiciary.\nL430@P16: The provisions about the executive emphasise its parliamentary character. Only members of\nL431@P16: Parliament may be Ministers of the Crown, members of the Executive Council, and Parliamentary\nL432@P16: Under-Secretaries. One Minister may also act for another.\nL433@P16: Parliament – the legislature – consists of the Sovereign and the House of Representatives.\nL434@P16: The members of the House are elected in accordance with the Electoral Act 1993. Each Parliament\nL435@P16: has a term of three years, unless it is earlier dissolved. The Governor-General has the power to\nL436@P16: summon, prorogue and dissolve Parliament. After each general election, Parliament is to meet\nL437@P16: within six weeks of the date fixed for the return of the writs.\nL438@P16: The Constitution Act recognises that Parliament continues to have full power to make laws; a Bill\nL439@P16: passed by the House becomes law when the Sovereign or Governor-General assents to it.\nL440@P16: The Constitution Act reaffirms the constitutional principles about parliamentary control of public\nL441@P16: finance: the Crown may not levy taxes, raise loans, or spend public money, except by or under an\nL442@P16-17: Act of Parliament.Cabinet Manual 2023\nL443@P17: 2 Introduction\nL444@P17: The provisions about the judiciary also relate back to long-established constitutional principle.\nL445@P17: To enhance their independence, the Judges of the Supreme Court, the Court of Appeal and the\nL446@P17: High Court are protected against removal from office and reduction of salary.\nL447@P17: Other major sources of the constitution\nL448@P17: The other major sources of the constitution include:\nL449@P17: • The prerogative powers of the Sovereign under which, for instance, the Queen\nL450@P17: issued the Letters Patent Constituting the Office of the Governor-General of\nL451@P17: New Zealand in 1983 and conferred her powers in respect of New Zealand on the\nL452@P17: Governor-General. The King appoints the Governor-General who, in general,\nL453@P17: exercises his prerogative powers. The King or Governor-General appoints and\nL454@P17: dismisses members of the Executive Council and Ministers of the Crown. Those\nL455@P17: powers are part of the common law. They exist independently of statutes, although\nL456@P17: statutes can, of course, limit or even supersede them.\nL457@P17: • Other relevant New Zealand statutes, such as the Public Service Act 2020, the\nL458@P17: Electoral Act 1993, the Senior Courts Act 2016, and the District Court Act 2016,\nL459@P17: relating in turn to the three branches of government, as well as the Ombudsmen\nL460@P17: Act 1975, the Official Information Act 1982, the Public Finance Act 1989 and the\nL461@P17: New Zealand Bill of Rights Act 1990.\nL462@P17: • Relevant English and United Kingdom statutes, such as Magna Carta 1297, the Bill\nL463@P17: of Rights 1688, and the Act of Settlement 1700 (regulating succession to the throne\nL464@P17: among other matters), all confirmed as part of the law of New Zealand by the Imperial\nL465@P17: Laws Application Act 1988. These statutes also regulate the relations between the state\nL466@P17: and the individual.\nL467@P17: • Relevant decisions of the courts, for instance decisions upholding rights of the\nL468@P17: individual against the powers of the state, and determining the extent of those powers.\nL469@P17: • The Treaty of Waitangi, which may indicate limits in our polity on majority decision\u0002making. The law sometimes accords a special recognition to Māori rights and interests,\nL470@P17: particularly those covered by Article 2 of the Treaty. And in many other cases the law\nL471@P17: and its processes should be determined by the general recognition in Article 3 of the\nL472@P17: Treaty that Māori belong, as citizens, to the whole community. In some situations,\nL473@P17: autonomous Māori institutions have a role within the wider constitutional and political\nL474@P17: system. In other circumstances, the model provided by the Treaty of Waitangi, of two\nL475@P17: parties negotiating and agreeing with one another, is appropriate. Policy and procedure\nL476@P17: in this area continues to evolve.\nL477@P17: • The conventions of the constitution, which in practice regulate, control and in some\nL478@P17: cases transform the use of the legal powers arising from the prerogative or conferred\nL479@P17: by statute. The most important conventions arise from the democratic character of our\nL480@P17: constitution. Constitutional conventions are of critical importance to the working of\nL481@P17: the constitution, even though they are not enforceable by the courts. In 1982, the\nL482@P17: Supreme Court of Canada summarised the constitutional position in that country in an\nL483@P17: equation: constitutional conventions plus constitutional law equal the total constitution\nL484@P17-18: of the country.Cabinet Manual 2023\nL485@P18: Introduction 3\nL486@P18: The underlying principle: Democracy\nL487@P18: The King reigns . . .\nL488@P18: This basic equation and the democratic character of the main conventions appear clearly in\nL489@P18: relation to the powers of the King and Governor-General under the law. Thus they may appoint\nL490@P18: Ministers and other holders of important offices (such as the Judges, the Defence Chiefs, the\n"
